1️⃣ Session vs JWT (Very Important)
| Feature     | Session               | JWT                  |
| ----------- | --------------------- | -------------------- |
| Storage     | Server                | Client               |
| State       | Stateful              | Stateless            |
| Scalability | Hard in microservices | Easy                 |
| Performance | Server memory used    | Lightweight          |
| Usage       | Traditional web apps  | Microservices / APIs |

Session Flow

User login

Server creates session id

Session stored in server memory

Browser stores session id in cookie

Each request sends session id

Problem:
If you have multiple servers, session sharing becomes difficult.

JWT Flow

User login

Server generates JWT Token

Token sent to client

Client sends token in Authorization header

Example:

Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...

Server verifies token without storing session.

That’s why JWT is used in microservices.

2️⃣ How Session Works Internally

Step-by-step flow:

Step 1 — User Login

User sends request

POST /login
username=abdullah
password=123
Step 2 — Server Creates Session

Server generates

SessionID = A7F8C9D2

Server stores:

SessionID -> {userId: 10, role: admin}
Step 3 — Cookie Sent to Browser
Set-Cookie: JSESSIONID=A7F8C9D2

Browser stores it.

Step 4 — Next Request

Browser automatically sends

Cookie: JSESSIONID=A7F8C9D2
Step 5 — Server Retrieves Session

Server checks memory

A7F8C9D2 -> user data

User is authenticated.

3️⃣ Session in Microservices

Sessions create problems in distributed systems.

Example architecture:

User
  |
Load Balancer
 |      |
Service1  Service2

If session stored in Service1 memory,
next request may go to Service2 → session lost.

Solutions:

1️⃣ Sticky Session

Load balancer always sends user to same server.

Problem → not scalable.

2️⃣ Shared Session Storage

Store session in:

Redis

Database

Memcached

Example:

User
  |
Load Balancer
 |      |
Service1  Service2
    |
   Redis

Both services access session.

3️⃣ JWT (Best Solution)

No session stored anywhere.

Server only verifies token.

That is why modern systems use JWT.

4️⃣ Session Fixation Attack

A security attack where attacker forces victim to use a known session id.

Example:

Attacker creates session

Sends link to victim

Victim logs in with same session id

Attacker now accesses victim account

Solution:

Regenerate session id after login

Example in Java:

request.changeSessionId();
5️⃣ Senior Java Interview Answer (Best)

If interviewer asks:

What is session?

You can say:

Session is a server-side mechanism used to maintain state across multiple HTTP requests.
 When a user logs in, the server creates a session object and assigns a unique session ID.
  This ID is stored in the client’s cookie and sent with every request so the server can identify the user.